issue_description:
# Feature request

## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.
After successful `CacheModule` implementation with Redis:

```
import { Module, CacheModule } from '@nestjs/common';
import * as redisStore from 'cache-manager-redis-store';

@Module({
    imports: [
        CacheModule.register({
            store: redisStore,
            host: 'localhost',
            port: 6379,
        }),
    ],
    controllers: [],
    providers: [],
})
export class AppModule {}
```

There is no way to handle upcoming errors. For example, if the connection to Redis server goes down, the whole application will crash:

```
<log> Error: Redis connection to 127.0.0.1:6379 failed - connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:6379
    at TCPConnectWrap.afterConnect [as oncomplete] (net.js:1113:14) </log>
```

## Possible solution
A method to get the underlying store to get the client. E.g.

```
redisCache.store.getClient();

redisClient.on('error', error => {
    <log> console.log(error); </log>
});
```

comment_bodies:
["# Feature request

## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.
After successful `CacheModule` implementation with Redis:

```
import { Module, CacheModule } from '@nestjs/common';
import * as redisStore from 'cache-manager-redis-store';

@Module({
    imports: [
        CacheModule.register({
            store: redisStore,
            host: 'localhost',
            port: 6379,
        }),
    ],
    controllers: [],
    providers: [],
})
export class AppModule {}
```

There is no way to handle upcoming errors. For example, if the connection to Redis server goes down, the whole application will crash:

```
<log> Error: Redis connection to 127.0.0.1:6379 failed - connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:6379
    at TCPConnectWrap.afterConnect [as oncomplete] (net.js:1113:14) </log>
```

## Possible solution
A method to get the underlying store to get the client. E.g.

```
let c = redisCache.store.getClient();

c.on('error', error => {
    <log> console.log(error) </log>;
});
```", "wrong project :D"]

["I prefer not to encourage the use of `Thread.sleep()` even in simple examples like this.
Instead, I'd be more comfortable stating in a comment something to the effect of:
```java
workerThread.start();

// Enqueue more jobs, etc.

// Shutdown the worker when finished
worker.end(true);
```", "Agree. Please update the example."]

