["Is it possible to enforce a rule where the cropped image isn't a perfect circle Example: "Oval shape" as it is possible to specify that in the viewport during cropping but the result returns a perfect circle which isn't in the same dimension as the viewport during cropping.", "Unfortunately no.  I don't think it'd be too difficult though.

Feel free to tinker around and submit a PR, I'd love to merge it in.", "On croppie.js line  979,  I changed a few things, added ctx.scale(1,2) and changed the arc arguments but only the top of the image is returned as an oval shape, the bottom of the image isn't. Pardon me for asking but what could be causing this issue? Thanks
`ctx.scale(1,2);
            ctx.beginPath();
            ctx.arc(outWidth / 2, outHeight / 2, outWidth *0.7 , 0, Math.PI * 2, true);
            ctx.closePath();`", "Ok, so after some work around within a busy schedule, I was able to get the oval shape but with the top cut off, which is the shape that I wanted  using this
 `ctx.fillStyle = '#fff';
 ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'destination-in';
ctx.scale(1,2);
 ctx.beginPath();
 ctx.arc(outWidth / 2, (outHeight/4)-5, (outHeight/4)+5, 0, Math.PI * 2, true);
 ctx.closePath();
ctx.fill();`

Another issue that I faced was that with some devices like android and Iphones, the original orientation wasn't set to portrait. I enabled ExIF which caused the final cropped image to be out of orientation and cropped the wrong portion of the image. So i disabled ExIf and used useragent detection to preset the orientation (might be a bit tacky but I'm so pressed for time at this point).
Also, the final image is zoomed in when displayed.
Don't know if this information will be useful but will just leave this here.", "@username_0 any news about this issue?
https://github.com/Foliotek/Croppie/issues/328"]
